Bullet 1: the app (Expo, Firebase, offline, reminders)

"Walk me through the app."

▎ "It tracks job, university and professor applications. Each type has its own stages and fields. Professors, for example, have a 'follow up due' stage, and I send a nudge automatically 7 days after emailing. It's Expo Router with TypeScript. Firebase Auth handles sign-in, and Realtime Database stores the data with per-user security rules: auth.uid === $uid."

"What does 'offline-tolerant writes' mean?"

▎ "Firebase writes update the local cache and the live listener immediately, but the write promise only resolves when the server confirms. I originally awaited that promise, so offline the form hung on 'Saving…' forever. Because push() generates the key on the client, I return the ID right away, don't await the server, and handle a server rejection separately with an alert. The tradeoff: the web Firebase SDK keeps pending writes in memory only, so they're lost if the app is killed while offline. Fixing that would need the native Firebase SDK."

"How do reminders work?"

▎ "Reminders are local notifications scheduled on the phone, re-synced whenever the data changes. Presets adapt to how far away the deadline is, and users can add custom times. On Android a reminder can also be an alarm: full-screen, looping sound, with Stop and Snooze. iOS caps pending notifications at 64, so only the soonest ones are scheduled."

