Bullet 2: the RAG pipeline

"Explain your RAG pipeline end to end."

▎ "There are two phases. Indexing: when an application changes, a background sync sends it to a Supabase Edge Function. The function splits the saved circular and notes into chunks, embeds each chunk with gemini-embedding-2 at 768 dimensions, and stores them in Postgres with pgvector. Query: the question is embedded with the query task type, a hybrid search finds the top 8 chunks for that user only, and Gemini answers from those excerpts, citing them as [1], [2]. The app shows only the sources the answer actually cited, and you can tap one to open that application."

"What chunking strategy, and why?"

▎ "Structure-aware chunking. Circulars already have sections like Responsibilities, Requirements and How to apply, so I split at those. Rather than having the LLM return the full text by section, which doubles output tokens, the extraction call returns only the headings, and code splits the text at them. A section over about 2,000 characters falls back to paragraphs, then lines, then sentences, with about 200 characters of overlap. Every chunk gets a label line on top, like 'Job: React Native Developer — TechNova, Section: Salary'. Without that, 'BDT 45,000' doesn't say which job it belongs to. I rejected fixed-size chunking because it cuts bullet points in half. I rejected semantic chunking because it costs extra embedding calls for little gain on documents that already have sections."

"Why 768 dimensions instead of 3072?"

▎ "A quarter of the storage, and faster search. The model is trained so a shortened vector keeps most of its quality. On a free-tier database that matters. I store the model name with every chunk, because vectors from different models can't be compared."

"Why hybrid search? What's RRF?"

▎ "Vector search finds meaning but misses exact terms like 'IELTS 6.5'. Keyword search finds exact words but misses paraphrases like 'pay' vs 'salary'. I run both in one Postgres function, take about 24 candidates from each, and merge them with reciprocal rank fusion: each chunk scores 1/(60 + rank) in each list. RRF uses ranks, so the two lists' different score scales don't matter. A chunk ranked well in both lists comes out on top."

"What's HNSW, and any problems with it?"

▎ "It's an approximate nearest-neighbour index: a layered graph, so search doesn't scan every vector. One known issue: it finds neighbours before my per-user filter is applied. With many users, a search could return fewer than 8 results. At scale I'd turn on pgvector's iterative index scans or partition by user. At one user's scale it's not a problem, and I documented it."

"What's hash-based incremental re-embedding?"

▎ "Each chunk gets a SHA-256 hash of the model, dimensions and text. On re-index, I compare against the stored hashes: new hashes get embedded and inserted, unchanged ones are kept, and missing ones are deleted after the inserts, so search never sees the application missing. Editing one note re-embeds one chunk, not all of them."

"How does the sync recover from being offline?"

▎ "The phone keeps a fingerprint of each application's searchable fields. After any change it waits 3 seconds for edits to settle, then re-indexes only the applications whose fingerprint changed, and removes deleted ones. If it fails, it just runs again on the next change or launch. On a fresh install there are no fingerprints, so it asks the server which applications are indexed and cleans up ones deleted on another device."

"Why not put everything in Supabase?"

▎ "Firebase already worked: live sync, offline writes, rules. So I made the vector store a derived index of Firebase. If Supabase were wiped, nothing would be lost, because the app rebuilds it. The cost is two databases to keep in sync, and I can't combine 'deadline this month' with vector similarity in one SQL query. Instead, I send the structured application list to the model alongside the retrieved excerpts, because dates and stages are facts in fields, not text to search."

