Bullet 3: latency and extraction

"How did you get from 19 s to 2.3 s?"

▎ "I benchmarked the same RAG prompt with the same three excerpts across models and settings. Gemini 3.5 Flash with default thinking took about 19 seconds. Its thinking level was the cause. At 'low', it took 2.3 seconds with the same complete, cited answer. Flash-Lite was faster, at about 1 second, but used only one of the three relevant excerpts, even after I changed the prompt. So I chose Flash at low thinking, the fastest option that kept answers complete."

Follow-up you should expect: "How many runs?"

▎ "Honestly, one run per configuration, on one question. That was enough to see a 19 s vs 2 s difference, but not to claim a precise number. Next I'd build an evaluation set of 20–30 questions with known correct chunks, and measure retrieval hit rate and answer faithfulness on every change."

Saying this proactively shows the judgment the job description asks for.

"How does the extraction work?"

▎ "Gemini Flash-Lite at temperature 0, with a response schema, so it must return JSON with fixed fields and type limited to three values. I send the user's UTC offset, so '30 November' becomes 23:59 in their time zone. Then I don't trust it. It also returns the exact sentence it took the deadline from, and if that sentence isn't in the text, the deadline is dropped. Links and emails must appear in the text too. Otherwise I fall back to the first URL or email a regex finds. Nothing saves automatically: the form is filled, and the user reviews and saves."

"Why use regex at all if you have an LLM?"

▎ "For URLs and emails, a regex is always right, instant and free. An LLM adds nothing there except the chance of being wrong. Part of the design was deciding where not to use AI: reminders, filters and 'due this week' are plain code too."

